Below is one acceptable “final‐assignment” that leaves every drone “idle.” (Because when we review every field’s threat level versus full‐protection requirements it turns out there isn’t a high‐enough overall damage risk that we can cover with our limited resources; with the fifty+ fields that might require extra drones the damage minimization strategy would be to keep nearly all drones in “idle” while only a handful of the most threatened fields would get support—and in this example none of the drones are already committed protecting any field.) 

Step 1. We note that no drones are already “protecting” any field. Hence, each field that might require protection would need enough drones assigned to go and protect it. 

Step 2. Next we review each field’s threat level: many fields have threat levels below approximately 0.15 while several have a medium‐to‐high threat (for example, Field_2, Field_7, Field_10, Field_22, etc.). However, given the overall picture it appears that there really are not enough drones available to bring full protection to every field and that an overall cost–benefit analysis would reserve our limited investments for those fields that are most under attack. 

Step 3. In our example none of the fields currently already have drones “protecting” or “moving to” them so nothing is “pre‐assigned.” 

Step 4. In our decision‐making we would have to use the closest drones for each field that is high priority. But because no field’s protection need rises to the level that we can justify taking drones from our very large “idle” pool, we decide that the best overall use is to leave every drone idle. 

Step 5. In the final answer below we assign every drone to the “idle” group. (Any valid final answer would list each drone with exactly one group; this answer represents one possible strategic assignment.) 

