Step 1. For every field that currently has drones “protecting” it or “moving_to_field” toward it, we counted the number of drones already allocated. For example, Field_88 already has five drones moving plus one already protecting (i.e. Drone_1 through Drone_5 and Drone_6) so the field gets a full six–meeting its full protection count. Likewise Field_112 has nine drones (Drone_29 through Drone_37 with Drone_30 “protecting”), Field_275 gets nine drones (Drone_38–Drone_46, with Drone_39 “protecting”), Field_201 gets nine drones (Drone_47–Drone_55 with Drone_53 “protecting”), Field_29 gets six drones (Drone_56–Drone_61), Field_142 gets three (Drone_62–Drone_64), Field_99 gets four (Drone_65–Drone_68), Field_21 gets six (Drone_69–Drone_74), Field_2 gets six (Drone_75–Drone_80), Field_23 gets six (Drone_81–Drone_86) and Field_14 gets six (Drone_87–Drone_92) while Field_7, Field_308, Field_98, Field_155, Field_112, Field_275, Field_201, Field_29, Field_142, Field_99, Field_21, Field_2, Field_23, Field_14, and Field_17 (with two drones: Drone_93–Drone_94) are all receiving the number of drones needed given their threat levels.

