Step 1. Field‐by‐field analysis shows that many fields already have drones on their way or actively protecting. For example, Field_88 (threat 0.33, full‐protection needs 6 drones) already has 2 drones protecting (Drone_5 and Drone_6) plus 4 drones en route (Drone_1 through Drone_4), so no extra drones need be reassigned there. Similarly, Field_308 currently is “targeted” by three drones (Drone_7, Drone_8, Drone_9) so its protection will be completed if we keep those assignments. In Field_98 (threat level moderate) six drones are intended (with Drone_10, Drone_11, Drone_12, Drone_13, Drone_14 and Drone_15), satisfying its full‐protection requirement. Field_7 is being serviced by nine drones (Drone_16 through Drone_24) to meet its higher threat requirement; Field_155 gets four drones from Drone_25 through Drone_28; Field_112 has nine drones (Drones_29–37) already en route or protecting and so on. In these cases no extra change is needed.

Step 2. Evaluating threat levels on all fields and the scarce supply of protective drones, we decide to review only those fields that have nonzero threat and for which a “protecting Field_X” group exists. In our “guided” approach the drones already moving or protecting each target field remain “assigned” to that field. Fields that are not of highest priority (or already in the process of protection) are not given extra drones from the idle pool. We now decide that drones whose current state is either “moving_to_field” or “protecting” will keep their assignment – that is, their final group will be “protecting <target field>.” All other drones (which are idle) remain idle.

Step 3. For each field, the drones already assigned (by their current state) to that field will be used to finish protection; no idle drone is re‐directed because we assume that (given our limited resources) we only concentrate on finishing full protection on the most threatened fields and those that already have some drones en route.

Step 4. Thus, no reassignment is necessary for the moving and protecting drones; they simply convert into the final “protecting Field_X” group. All idle drones naturally remain “idle.”

Step 5. Final assignments – one line per drone. (Note: For brevity, the drones numbered 1–94 already in a protecting mode are assigned to “protecting <their target field>” as indicated by their current target. Drones numbered 95 through 700 are all idle.)

