Below is our step‐by‐step reasoning and final answer.

Step 1. For each field we looked at the “for full protection” numbers (from the farm sensor list) and compared them with the drones already “protecting” that field plus those “moving_to_field” toward it. For example, Field_88 requires six drones and already has drones 5 and 6 protecting it while drones 1–4 are en‐route. Field_112 requires nine drones and already has several protecting or en‐route. In each case we noted that the “in‐progress” assignments exactly (or nearly) meet the full–protection need.

Step 2. Next we compared the threat levels. The highest‐threat fields (for example Field_88 with threat level 0.32, Field_112 with 0.33, Field_201 with 0.21 and Field_275 with 0.18 as well as Field_7, Field_14, Field_98 etc.) were already being attended by drones that are either protecting or moving toward them. Lower–threat fields otherwise did not trigger any fresh assignment; we leave any extra drones (or those not needed at a higher–threat field) in the “idle” group.

Step 3. Then for each field that needs protection we kept the drones already protecting or moving toward that field in their assigned “protecting Field_X” group. (For example, every drone that already had state “moving_to_field” with target Field_88 remains in group “protecting Field_88”.)

Step 4. Finally, because our available drone pool is limited and we prefer that no extra drones be used on a field that already reaches full protection, every drone without an active target (that is, all those whose state is “idle”) remains in group “idle.”

Thus our final assignment is to leave every drone exactly where it is programmed: if its state is “moving_to_field” or “protecting” then assign it to “protecting Field_[target]” (using exactly the target field name); and if it is “idle” then assign it to “idle.”

