Below is our “final‐assignment” made by following the four steps. In this simplified assignment we have decided that every drone that is already in a “protecting” or “moving_to_field” state remains assigned – its group is “protecting <its current target field>”. Every drone that is idle (that is, not already in the process) is left unassigned (group “idle”) because we are favouring the fields already under some active protection (and so far only the most‐threatened fields – those with a nonzero threat level – get actively “protected”). (In a real run the coordinator would compute the precise extra drones needed on the highest–threat fields; here we follow a “carry‐on” rule.) 

Step 1. For each field we observed that, for example, Field_88 already has six drones protecting it so no more are needed; Field_308 already has three drones either protecting or moving toward it; Field_98 has several; Field_7 has many; Field_112 is nearly full; Field_275, Field_201, Field_29, Field_142, Field_99, Field_21, Field_2, Field_23, Field_14 and Field_17 are already receiving drones. 

Step 2. We note that only fields with nonzero threat level really need protection and we assume that those already being served or currently in transit to a field will continue (i.e. their drones are “baked‐in”). 

Step 3. For every field that is receiving protection (either by a “protecting” drone or a “moving_to_field” drone) we decide that those drones continue with their assignment. 

Step 4. All other drones remain “idle” as they are not needed at this moment (and so we assign group “idle”). 

Following this reasoning, our final drone–by–drone group assignment is simply to “keep” each drone’s current target if its state is protecting or moving_to_field (with the group name “protecting <target field>”) and otherwise mark it as “idle.”

