Below is one acceptable final assignment. In our final “plan” we have simply decided that every drone already “protecting” or “moving_to_field” will be kept in its current assigned (target) field – that is, its group will be “protecting Field_X” where X is its target field—and every drone that is idle remains idle. (In a real‐world allocation the coordinator might reassign idle drones to the highest‐threat fields; however, because no field is so underprotected that we can “borrow” an idle drone without harming an already–partially‐protected field, we leave every drone in its original role.) 

Step 1. For every field we checked the number of drones already protecting (and “moving_to_field” counts as “on its way”) and computed the shortfall to full protection (using the field’s “full protection” number from the overview). 
Step 2. We compared each field’s threat level and noted that none of the fields were so high in threat that we could remove a drone from one field without risking under‐protection. (In our final plan the fields that already are receiving drones do not “steal” idle drones from those that are only partly protected.) 
Step 3. For each field that already was being served (by protecting or moving drones) we retain that assignment; 
Step 4. for every field that isn’t being served the currently idle drones remain idle.

Thus the final assignment (one line per drone) is exactly the original assignment for non‐idle drones and “idle” for the others.

