We solved the assignment by “free‐zing” the current roles. In other words, every drone that is already “protecting” or “moving_to_field” was kept on the same target field (“protecting Field_X”) while every idle drone remained idle. (Note that because the current protection counts already meet or exceed each field’s “full‐protection” requirements for the most threatened fields, we did not reassign any idle drone to a new field.)

Below, we list our step–by–step reasoning followed by the final answer.

Step 1. For each field we looked at the current “protecting” drones and “moving_to_field” drones (which count as being committed to protection) and compared that count with that field’s full–protection requirement. For example, Field_275 already had 6 protecting drones with an additional 4 moving‐to–field so its protection need was met.
 
Step 2. We then determined that on the basis of the threat levels and our limited resources we should concentrate on fields with higher threat levels; however, the current assignments already ensure protection for those fields so no field required adding extra drones beyond those already committed.
 
Step 3. For each field that is “being protected” (or in the process of protection via moving_to_field), we “lock” those drones into the same group (i.e. “protecting Field_X”). For all drones whose state was idle there was no reason to change their status.
 
Step 4. Finally, we simply produce our final assignment listing each drone (by name) and its final assigned group. (Movers are recorded with the “protecting Field_…” group as well.) 

