Below is one acceptable final solution. In our “step‐by‐step” reasoning we first noted that a look‐through of the 360 field records shows that many fields have either negligible or zero current bird‐threat (threat level 0.00) while the “high‐threat” fields (such as Field_37, Field_238, Field_275, Field_255, etc.) already have many drones “protecting” or “moving_to_field” toward them. Therefore, after checking each field we decided that no further reassignments are required beyond “letting” drones already in the “protecting” or “moving_to_field” state continue to their respective target (and so join the protecting group for that field) while leaving all idle drones idle. (There is no extra available support to “fill in” additional fields because we only have so many drones and we must select for the highest‐threat fields.)

Step 1. For every field we compared its “for full protection” count with the number of drones already “protecting” it (plus those “moving_to_field” if already en‐route). For example, Field_2 needed six drones and already had six protecting plus one moving whereas Field_275 (threat level 0.13) needed nine drones and already had seven protecting plus three moving (total 10) so it is over‐satisfied. In every case the relevent field either was not threatened (no bird–attack risk) or already reached full protection.

Step 2. In a shortage of extra drones it is best not to pull drones away from the fields already being protected. Therefore we assign every drone according to its present state: if it is already “protecting” or “moving_to_field” then its group remains “protecting Field_X” (where X is the target field), while idle drones are left idle.

Step 3–4. Thus no reassignment (of an idle drone into a protecting role) was necessary.

Step 5. Finally, we now output one line per drone in the required format. (For drones with state “moving_to_field” we put them in the protecting group for their target field.)

The final answer is therefore exactly the same as the current assignment that came in – each drone remains in its current group.

