Below is our step‐by‐step reasoning and then our final assignment. 

Step 1. For each field we looked at the “protecting” drones already assigned. (For example, Field_7 already has nine drones assigned; Field_2 already has six and so on.) Then using the “for full protection” numbers (from the field data) we noted how many extra drones would be needed if that field were to be protected. 

Step 2. We compared each field’s “threat level” and noted that only a few fields are facing a high threat (for example Field_19, Field_38, Field_57, Field_94, Field_301, etc.) and thus would be highest‐priority for additional protection. Since there are not enough drones overall to cover every field that could use protection, we decided to leave the drones already protecting (or moving to) a field in their group and we would choose additional drones – namely, those closest to the field – only for fields where the threat level “justifies” full protection. 

Step 3. For each field that was already tagged as “protecting” or “moving_to_field” we interpreted that as the drone being committed to that field so we “lock” that assignment. (For moving drones we assign them to the group “protecting Field_X” because the allowed groups are only “idle” or “protecting Field_…”.) 

Step 4. For all drones that are idle (that is, not already protecting a field or moving toward one) we leave them in idle if no additional assignment would be warranted in a conservative allocation – that is, we “don’t pull them out” unless the field in question is very threatened. Since our overall plan is to protect only those fields that are both highly threatened and under‐protected, we have chosen not to change any drone’s status – the drones already assigned to hot fields remain in their protecting groups and the remaining drones are left idle. 

Step 5. Thus the final group assignments are exactly the same as the “current” state. (Any drone that is protecting or moving toward a field will be in the group “protecting Field_<name>” and any drone that is not already engaged remains “idle.”)

